  {
    "episode_id": 51684,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/5/1/51684.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 37,
    "turn_text": "No, well, I supported against Afghanistan, and I believe we should have been able to keep a number of troops there. The reason I say that is not people call it an endless war, what it really was, I mean, that was K-1, Taliban and al-Qaeda. still active in Afghanistan. It's on the Pakistan border. It's I think the airport at Kabul is only 300 miles from China. It's a tremendous amount of minerals there in Afghanistan. But also Pakistan to me is a real looming threat. They have nuclear weapons. They have Islamists in their government. I agree. Alarmists don't control the government, but they're in the government. And us being in Afghanistan, we used to monitor, we used to know what was going on, we can follow You know, the nuclear weapons we could see if there were incursions being made. So, no, it took on a different form as it went over the years. I didn't look upon it as a nation building. I looked at it upon it as us having an outpost there where we can monitor what was being done, one by China, secondly by the Islamists, and also quite frankly in Russia. I mean, that's really a key location. I think things were done wrong over the years. I think, for instance, when Obama, I guess it was in 2009, He said he was going to increase the troop levels and really fight back, but then he put a deadline on it, which meant just the Taliban just waited to let the deadline run up. No, in 2001, when we, it was really the short term was to crush the Taliban, crush al-Qaeda, but then as we looked at it, we saw that they were still there in the mountains that were still active in Pakistan, coming in and out of Pakistan. No, but no one I think could have foreseen what was or did or claimed that they knew what was going to happen over the next 20 years.",
